  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 523 PM EST TUE NOV 3 2026\n\nAn amplified long wave pattern will be in store over the CONUS \nthrough early next week. A positively tilted trough will start out \naligned from the Great Lakes through the southern Plains, with an \nassociated dampening surface low positioned over Lake Superior. The \nupper trough will gradually migrate eastward, with a cutoff low \nlikely emerging over the Great Lakes. An associated surface cold \nfront will cross the Commonwealth on Friday. Troughing will be \nreinforced east of the Mississippi River, as additional short wave\nenergy traverses southeast from the central CONUS to the \nSoutheast within the southern stream. In the northern stream, the \ntroughing will be bolstered by energy translating across \nsoutheastern Canada. At the surface, broader high pressure will \nmigrate south from the Midwest this weekend to the lower \nMississippi Valley by early next week, with cooler and drier \nconditions seen across the Ohio and Tennessee valleys. \n\nSensible weather will feature one more day of 70s for highs across \nmost of eastern Kentucky on Thursday. Generally dry weather is \nexpected, besides just enough lingering low level moisture that \ncould promote some light rain showers early across our southeast. As \na secondary cold front approaches late Thursday night into \nFriday, some light rain chances may be renewed across our \nsoutheast, although model guidance has been trending drier, so \nthis may be more of a temporary increase in clouds only. Cooler \nand drier conditions will then be the story as we head into the \nweekend, with highs returning to the upper 50s and lower 60s, \nwhile lows drop back into the 30s. Cloud cover will dictate how \ncold the valleys get both Saturday and Sunday nights. At this \ntime, Sunday night looks like the colder night, with some of the \ndeeper valleys dipping to near or just below the freezing mark. \nMore modest return flow ensues by next Tuesday, with highs \nmoderating to the lower and middle 60s. Generally dry weather \nwill also continue to hold on through that time.",
